-- Passage rg10-authors-purpose-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('6c81c09c-9abe-5798-b04e-1be1002118ed', 'rg10-authors-purpose-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-authors-purpose'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Why We Leave the Fading Alone$q$, $q$From a post by Hyun-woo Park, head textile conservator at the Carrow Valley Historical Museum, answering visitors' questions about a quilt on display.

Every few weeks, a visitor stops me in the gallery and asks the same question about our 1870s wedding quilt. Why don't we fix it? The reds have faded to a soft pink, one corner is worn thin, and a brown stain spreads across the lower border. Surely, the visitor suggests, a museum with skilled staff could make it look new again.

We could. With modern dyes and new thread, we could restore the colors and patch the corner so neatly that few people would notice. But we would be erasing the very thing that makes this quilt worth seeing.

Consider that worn corner. It is thin because someone pulled the quilt up to their chin on cold nights for decades. Consider the stain. Our tests suggest it is tea, perhaps spilled during one of the many winters this quilt spent on a farmhouse bed. Consider the faded reds, which tell us which side faced the window. Each flaw is a piece of evidence about the people who lived with this object.

A conservator's job is not to make things look new. It is to keep them from getting worse. So we clean the quilt gently, store it flat in the dark between exhibitions, and limit how long it hangs under gallery lights. Where the fabric is splitting, we stitch it to a backing of fine net so it will not tear further. Every step we take can be undone by a future conservator who knows more than we do.

I admit that a restored quilt would photograph beautifully. It would also be a kind of costume, an object dressed up as something it never was. When you visit, I hope you will look closely at the pink that was once red and the corner worn soft by human hands. That is not damage. That is history.$q$, 'draft')
