-- Passage rg10-authors-purpose-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('3afc60df-1469-543f-8804-643b3b3788c0', 'rg10-authors-purpose-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-authors-purpose'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Notebook Keepers$q$, $q$From an article by historian Joaquin Reyes for a county historical society journal.

In the winter of 1883, a schoolteacher named Hannah Lindqvist walked to the edge of her schoolyard every morning at seven o'clock, read a thermometer nailed to a fence post, and wrote the number in a small cloth notebook. She did the same at two in the afternoon and again at nine at night. She was not paid. She did it for twenty-six years.

Hannah was one of hundreds of volunteer weather observers scattered across the region in the late 1800s. Farmers, shopkeepers, postmasters, and railroad clerks kept daily records of temperature, rainfall, and wind. They mailed their notebooks to a central office, where clerks copied the numbers into large ledgers.

Most histories of weather science focus on inventors and professors, the people who designed instruments or published theories. Those people matter. Yet theories need data, and for decades the data came from people like Hannah, standing in the cold with a pencil.

Why should we remember them now? Because their notebooks are still in use. When scientists today want to know how much the region's rainfall has changed over the past century and a half, they turn to these records. A theory can be revised in an afternoon. A missing day of observations can never be recovered. Every reading that Hannah took, on mornings when she surely would have preferred to stay inside, is a fact no one else can supply.

The volunteers rarely signed their work with more than initials, and few of their names appear in any textbook. That is a gap worth closing. The next time you read about how the weather of a place has changed, picture a fence post, a thermometer, and a teacher with a notebook. The story of science is not only a story of brilliant ideas. It is also a story of patience.$q$, 'draft')
